# Environment for the Raid Clash Tracker. Copy this file to .env.local (which is
# gitignored) and fill in your own values. Never commit a real connection string —
# it contains your database password.

# The one and only database: a Supabase Postgres project, reached over its POOLED
# connection on port 6543. Shape only — replace every <ANGLE-BRACKET> placeholder:
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<PROJECT-REF>:<YOUR-DB-PASSWORD>@<PREFIX>-<REGION>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres

# Don't assemble the host by hand. The pooler hostname is NOT just your region —
# it carries a platform prefix in front of it (`aws-0-<region>`, `aws-1-<region>`,
# ...), so substituting a bare region gives you a hostname that doesn't resolve.
# Copy the whole URI verbatim from your Supabase project's connection panel (take
# the transaction-pooler / port-6543 string) and substitute only your password.
# A host that doesn't resolve is NOT the demo case: it lands on the error page,
# which is correct but tells you nothing about the typo.

# Port 6543 is the transaction pooler, not the direct 5432 connection. The pooler
# does not support prepared statements, so the client in lib/db.ts is created with
# `prepare: false`, which turns them off. Leave prepared statements ON (postgres.js
# enables them by default) and queries fail intermittently.
#
# If your password contains any of : / ? # [ ] @ % it must be percent-encoded here.
#
# Apply the schema with `npm run db:migrate` (it applies supabase/migrations/*.sql
# in order and is safe to re-run), then optionally `npm run seed` for sample data.

# Leave DATABASE_URL unset — or skip .env.local entirely — and the dashboard
# renders the bundled demo dataset instead of live data. Any *other* connection
# problem surfaces as an error page: demo numbers are never shown as if real.
# A blank or whitespace-only value counts as unset, so commenting the line out and
# emptying it behave the same.

# ── How the connection string is resolved (lib/db.ts `databaseUrl()`) ──────────
#
# First NON-BLANK of these two wins:
#
#   1. DATABASE_URL   what you set here / in .env.local, in CI, and what every
#                     script in scripts/ uses. Explicit always beats inherited,
#                     so setting it locally or on a host overrides the platform.
#   2. POSTGRES_URL   created automatically by the Supabase<->Vercel integration.
#                     You do NOT need to add DATABASE_URL to a Vercel project —
#                     the integration's POSTGRES_URL is picked up as-is. Set
#                     DATABASE_URL there only to point at a different database.
#
# Neither set (or both blank) -> demo mode, exactly as before.
#
# The integration also creates two more connection strings. NEITHER IS EVER READ,
# on purpose — they are named here because they are the first thing you will reach
# for when a deployment cannot connect:
#
#   POSTGRES_URL_NON_POOLING  the DIRECT connection on 5432, bypassing the pooler.
#                             On serverless it opens one real Postgres connection
#                             per invocation, so a burst of page renders exhausts
#                             the project's connection limit and the site errors.
#   POSTGRES_PRISMA_URL       the pooled endpoint with Prisma-specific query
#                             parameters (connection_limit, pgbouncer=...) added.
#                             This app uses postgres.js, not Prisma; those
#                             parameters mean nothing to it.
#
# Port 6543 is expected but VERIFIED, not assumed: if the resolved URI is on any
# other port, lib/db.ts logs one line naming the port and the variable it came
# from, and carries on. It never throws, and it never logs the URI — that would
# publish the password.
#
# Three caveats on that line, so its absence is not read as a pass:
#
#   * An UNPARSEABLE URI logs NOTHING AT ALL. A typo'd string that `new URL()`
#     rejects produces silence, not a warning — postgres.js fails on its own
#     terms instead. So "no port warning" does not mean "the URI is fine".
#   * The check runs ONCE PER PROCESS, not once per resolution or per render (a
#     globalThis flag; it must survive Next's dev HMR or it would repeat on every
#     reload). Restart the process to see it again.
#   * On serverless that means once per WARM INSTANCE, i.e. at most once per cold
#     start. Not finding the line in a log tail is NOT proof the port is right.
#
# Beware POSTGRES_URL's generic name. If your environment already exports it for
# something unrelated — another Postgres/Neon integration, a docker-compose
# database, a shell profile you keep for psql — this app will now use THAT
# database, and if it happens to be migrated your writes land there with no
# error. Set DATABASE_URL explicitly wherever you can; it wins the chain.


# ── Admin login (reads are public; every write is admin-only) ──────────────────
#
# All three are SERVER-ONLY. Note there is deliberately no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix on
# any of them: sign-in runs entirely in a Server Action, so no Supabase client is
# ever created in the browser and the key never enters the client bundle.
#
# Find both Supabase values in your project's API settings. Use the PUBLISHABLE
# key (`sb_publishable_...`) — never the secret key (`sb_secret_...`), which
# bypasses every protection the database has.
SUPABASE_URL=https://<PROJECT-REF>.supabase.co
SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_<REST-OF-YOUR-KEY>

# The single account allowed to write. Compared case-insensitively against the
# signed-in user's verified email claim, so it must match the user you created in
# Supabase → Authentication → Users exactly.
ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com

# WHAT HAPPENS WHEN THESE ARE MISSING — every case fails CLOSED:
#   ADMIN_EMAIL unset or blank .... nobody is admin; all seven write routes 401 and
#                                   the UI shows no write controls at all.
#   SUPABASE_URL / KEY missing .... same, plus /login explains it is not
#                                   configured rather than erroring. Reads keep
#                                   working: the dashboard stays fully public.
# A misconfiguration therefore locks writing, never opens it. If your data is
# readable but you cannot sign in, suspect these three before suspecting the code.
#
# Keep signups DISABLED in Supabase (Authentication → Sign In / Providers). That
# is what makes ADMIN_EMAIL meaningful — without it, anyone could register an
# account, and only the email comparison would stand between them and your data.
